\label{def:dirlag}
A subspace $\mathcal D \subset \mathcal X \times \mathcal X^*$ is called a \emph{Dirac structure} if the bilinear form $\langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle_+$ is zero on $\mathcal D$ and, moreover, $\mathcal D$ is maximal with respect to this property. A subspace $\mathcal L \subset \mathcal X \times \mathcal X^*$ is called a \emph{Lagrange structure}\footnote{Normally called a Lagrangian subspace. However, we adopt the terminology Lagrange structure in order to stress the similarity with Dirac structures.}
if the bilinear form $\langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle_-$ is zero on $\mathcal L$ and, moreover, $\mathcal L$ is maximal with respect to this property. Furthermore, a Lagrange structure $\mathcal L$ is called \emph{nonnegative} if the bilinear form $\Sigma_+$ is nonnegative whenever restricted to $\mathcal L$.

\label{def:defph}
A \emph{linear port-Hamiltonian DAE system} (pHDAE) on a linear state space $\mathcal{X}$ is defined by a pair $(\mathcal{D},\mathcal{L})$, where $\mathcal{D} \subset \mathcal{X}\times \mathcal{X}^*$ is a Dirac structure, and $\mathcal{L} \subset {\mathcal{X}} \times \mathcal{X}^*$ is a Lagrange structure. Its dynamics is given by the relation
\begin{equation}\label{phdef}
\{(x,\dot{x}) \mid \exists\, e \in \mathcal{X}^* \mbox{ s.t. } (x,e) \in \mathcal{L}, (-\dot{x},e) \in \mathcal{D} \}.
\end{equation}

\label{def:defnlph}
A port-Hamiltonian system on a state manifold $\mathcal{X}$ is defined by a pair $(\mathcal{D},\mathcal{L})$, where $\mathcal{D}$ is a Dirac structure on $\mathcal{X}$ (i.e., a subbundle of $T\mathcal{X} \oplus T^*\mathcal{X}$), and $\mathcal{L} \subset T^*\mathcal{X}$ is a Lagrangian submanifold. Its dynamics is given by the relation

\begin{equation*}
\{(x,\dot{x}) \in T\mathcal{X} \mid\ \exists\, e \ \in T_x^* \mathcal{X} \mbox{ s.t. } (x,e) \in \mathcal{L}, (-\dot{x},e) \in \mathcal{D}(x) \}.
\end{equation*}

\label{singular}
{\rm Consider the optimal control problem of minimizing a cost functional $\int_{t_0}^{t_f}  K(q(t),u(t)) dt$ for a control system $\dot{q}=f(q,u)$, with $q: [t_0,t_f]\to  \mathbb{R}^n$, $u [t_0,t_f]\to \mathbb{R}^m$, $q(t_0) \in \mathbb{R}^n$. Define the \emph{optimal control Hamiltonian}

\begin{equation*}
\mathcal H(q,p,u)=  p^\top f(q,u) - K(q,u) ,
\end{equation*}

with $p \in \mathbb{R}^n$ the co-state vector. Application of Pontryagin's Maximum principle leads to the consideration of the port-Hamiltonian DAE system on the space with coordinates $(q,p,u)$, given as

\begin{equation}
\label{o1}
\begin{bmatrix} I & 0 & 0 \\[2mm] 0 & I & 0 \\[2mm] 0 & 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{q} \\[2mm] \dot{p} \\[2mm] \dot{u}
\end{bmatrix} =
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & I_n & 0 \\[2mm]
-I_n & 0 & 0 \\[2mm]
0 & 0 & I_m
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\frac{\partial \mathcal H}{\partial q}(q,p,u) \\[2mm]
\frac{\partial \mathcal H}{\partial p}(q,p,u) \\[2mm]
\frac{\partial \mathcal h}{\partial u}(q,p,u)
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation}

The underlying Dirac structure is given as

\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{D} =\left \{ \left( \begin{bmatrix} f_q \\[2mm] f_p \\[2mm] f_u \end{bmatrix} , \begin{bmatrix} e_q \\[2mm] e_p \\[2mm] e_u \end{bmatrix}\right) \mid f_q = - e_p, \, f_p = e_q, \, e_u=0\right \} ,
\end{equation*}

i.e., the direct product of the Dirac structure on the $(q,p)$ space given by the graph of the canonical skew-symmetric map $\begin{bmatrix} 0 & -I \\ I & 0 \end{bmatrix}$ from $\begin{bmatrix} e_q \\ e_p \end{bmatrix}$ to $\begin{bmatrix} f_q \\ f_p \end{bmatrix}$, with the trivial Dirac structure $\{(f_u,e_u) \mid e_u=0 \}$. The resulting Dirac algebraic constraints are $\frac{\partial \mathcal H}{\partial u}(q,p,u) =0$.

System \eqref{o1} can be equivalently rewritten as a port-Hamiltonian system only involving the $(q,p)$ variables, with {\it implicit} energy storage relation given by the Lagrangian submanifold

\begin{equation}
\label{o2}
\mathcal{L} = \left \{\left(
\begin{bmatrix}
q \\[2mm] p
\end{bmatrix},
\begin{bmatrix}
e_q \\[2mm] e_p
\end{bmatrix} \right)
\mid
\exists\, u \mbox{ s.t. }
\begin{bmatrix}
e_q \\[2mm] e_p
\end{bmatrix}  =
\begin{bmatrix}
\frac{\partial \mathcal{H}}{\partial q}(q,p,u) \\[2mm]
\frac{\partial \mathcal{H}}{\partial p}(q,p,u)
\end{bmatrix} , \; \frac{\partial \mathcal{H}}{\partial u}(q,p,u)=0.
\right \}
\end{equation}

Thus, the optimal control Hamiltonian $\mathcal H(q,p,u)$ defines a Morse family for $\mathcal{L}$ (with $u$ taking the role of the Lagrange multipliers), and the conversion of \eqref{o2} into \eqref{o1} is an example of conversion of a Lagrange algebraic constraint into a Dirac algebraic constraint, see  \cite{SchM20} for details.
}

{\rm
The developments in this subsection also form the starting point for the theory of \emph{Dirac brackets}, as developed in the 1950s by Paul Dirac, see e.g. \cite{Dir58}. In this context the constraints $c(x)=0$ arise from non-surjectivity of the map $(q,\dot{q}) \mapsto (q, p= \frac{\partial l}{\partial \dot{q}}(q,\dot{q}))$ from $TQ$ to $T^*Q$, for a singular Lagrangian function $L(q,\dot{q})$. This means that the Hamiltonian is $\mathcal H(x) + \mu^\top c(x)$, where $\mu$ is undetermined. By possibly differentiating the \emph{primary} constraints $c(x)=0$ to \emph{secondary} constraints one is led to a constraint submanifold of the state space $\mathcal{X} = T^*Q$. Under a regularity assumption (the decoupling matrix has full rank), the system \eqref{explicit2} restricted to this constraint manifold defines a unique Hamiltonian vector field  with respect to a Poisson bracket that is called the Dirac bracket. See \cite{NvdS90} (Chapter 12) for details.
}

{\rm The
Livens principle can be regarded as a special case of the Pontryagin maximum principle \cite{BolGMP62} in optimal control (as was already indicated in \cite{KinBS23}). In fact, consider the optimal control problem of minimizing a cost criterion $\int K(x(t),u(t) dt$ with respect to dynamics $\dot{x}=f(x,u)$; see Example \ref{singular}. Then the first-order necessary conditions of Pontryagin's maximum principle amount to the port-Hamiltonian DAE system

\begin{equation}
\label{optimal}
\begin{bmatrix} I & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & I & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}
 \begin{bmatrix} \dot{x} \\ \dot{p} \\ \dot{u} \end{bmatrix}
 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & I & 0 \\ -I & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & I\end{bmatrix}
 \begin{bmatrix}  \frac{\partial \mathcal H_{opt}}{\partial q} \\  \frac{\partial \mathcal H_{opt}}{\partial p} \\ \frac{\partial \mathcal H_{opt}}{\partial u} \end{bmatrix},
 \end{equation}

with $\mathcal H_{opt}(x,u,p) := p^\top f(x,u) - L(x,u)$ being the \emph{optimal control Hamiltonian}. Thus, seen from this angle the Livens principle corresponds to the special case $f(x,u)=u$. All this is closely related to the observation made in \cite{suss97}, where \eqref{optimal} is referred to as 'Hamilton's equations as he should have written them'. Said otherwise, from our point of view 'Hamilton's equations are actually port-Hamiltonian DAEs'.
}

A nonlinear dissipative port-Hamiltonian DAE system is defined by a triple $(\mathcal{D},\mathcal{L},\mathcal{R})$, where
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\mathcal{D}$ is a Dirac structure
\begin{equation*} \label{diracstruct}
\mathcal{D} (x) \subset T_x\mathcal{X} \times T^*_x \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{F}_R \times \mathcal{E}_R \times \mathcal{F}_P \times \mathcal{E}_P, \quad x \in \mathcal{X},
\end{equation*}
with $\mathcal{X}$ the state space manifold, $\mathcal{F}_R$ and $\mathcal{E}_R=\mathcal{F}_R^*$ are dual flow and effort linear spaces corresponding to energy dissipation, and $\mathcal{F}_P$ and $\mathcal{E}_P=\mathcal{F}_P^*$ are dual flow and effort linear spaces corresponding to external ports,
\item
$\mathcal{L}$ is a Lagrangian submanifold

\begin{equation*} \label{lagrangestruct}
\mathcal{L} \subset T^*\mathcal{X}
\end{equation*}

corresponding to energy storage, and
\item
$\mathcal{R}$ is a resistive relation defined by a submanifold

\begin{equation*} \label{resistiverelation}
\mathcal{R} \subset \mathcal{F}_R \times \mathcal{E}_R, \quad \mbox{ such that }e_R^\top f_R \geq 0,\ \mbox{ for all}\ (f_R,e_R) \in \mathcal{R},
\end{equation*}

with $\dim \mathcal{R} = \dim \mathcal{F}_R$.
\end{enumerate}
The resulting port-Hamiltonian DAE system is defined by the equations
\begin{equation}
\label{pHDAE}
\begin{array}{c}
(-\dot{x}(t), e(t), f_R(t),e_R(t),f_P(t),e_P(t)) \in \mathcal{D} (x(t)), \\[2mm]
(-f_R(t),e_R(t)) \in \mathcal{R}, \quad (x(t),e(t)) \in \mathcal{L}, \; t \in \mathbb{R}.
\end{array}
\end{equation}

\label{prop:monotone0}
A subspace $\mathcal{M} \subset \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X}^*$ is a monotone structure if and only if
\begin{equation*}
\label{mono}
<e \mid f>\,  \geq 0
\end{equation*}
for all $(f,e) \in \mathcal{M}$, and a maximally monotone structure if additionally $\mathcal{M}$ is maximal with respect to this property. Any monotone structure $\mathcal{M}$ satisfies $\dim \mathcal{M} \leq \dim \mathcal{X}$ and is a maximally monotone structure if and only if $\dim \mathcal{M} = \dim \mathcal{X}$.
Furthermore, the graph $\{(Me,e) \in \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X}^* \mid e \in \mathcal{X}^* \}$ of a linear map
$M: \mathcal{X}^* \to \mathcal{X}$ is a maximally monotone structure if and only if $M$ is monotone.

\label{prop:decompose}
Consider linear spaces $\mathcal{F}_R$ and $\mathcal{E}_R=\mathcal{F}_R^*$ such that $\mathcal{M} = \mathcal{D} \circ \mathcal{R}$ for some  Dirac structure $\mathcal{D} \subset \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X}^* \times \mathcal{F}_R \times \mathcal{E}_R$ and a nonnegative Lagrange structure $\mathcal{R} \subset \mathcal{F}_R \times \mathcal{E}_R$. Then $\mathcal{M}$ is a maximally monotone structure.

Conversely, let $\mathcal{M} \subset \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X}^*$ be a maximally monotone structure. Then there exist linear spaces $\mathcal{F}_R$ and $\mathcal{E}_R=\mathcal{F}_R^*$ such that $\mathcal{M} = \mathcal{D} \circ \mathcal{R}$ for some  Dirac structure $\mathcal{D} \subset \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X}^* \times \mathcal{F}_R \times \mathcal{E}_R$ and some nonnegative Lagrange structure $\mathcal{R} \subset \mathcal{F}_R \times \mathcal{E}_R$.

\label{prop:diracaug}
Let $\mathcal{X}$ be a finite-dimensional linear space. Consider any Dirac structure $\mathcal{D} \subset \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X}^*$, and suppose that $\pi^*(\mathcal{D}) \subset \mathcal{X}^*$ is $(n-k)$-dimensional, where $\pi^*: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X}^* \to \mathcal{X}^*$ is the projection onto the second argument. With $\Lambda:= \mathbb{R}^k$,  there exists a full-rank $n \times k$ map $C_\lambda :\Lambda^* \to \mathcal{X}$ and a skew-symmetric map $J: \mathcal{X}^* \to \mathcal{X} $, such that $\mathcal{D}$ is given as

\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{D} = \{(f,e) \in \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X}^* \mid\ \exists\ e_\lambda \in \Lambda^* \mbox{ s.t. } -f = Je + C_\lambda e_\lambda,\, 0 = C_\lambda^\top e \}.
\end{equation*}

In fact, one can take any $C_\lambda$ such that $\ker C_\lambda^\top = \pi^* (\mathcal{D})$. Conversely, any such equations for a skew-symmetric map $J: \mathcal{X}^* \to \mathcal{X}$ define a Dirac structure.

\label{prop:lagrangeaug}
For any Lagrange structure $\mathcal{L}\in  \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X}^*$ there exists a full-rank $n \times k$ matrix $C_\mu$ and a symmetric $n \times n$ matrix $Q$ such that

\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{L} = \{(x,e) \in \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X}^* \mid\ \exists \mu \in \mathbb{R}^k \mbox{ s.t. }e =  Qx + C_\mu \mu, 0  =  C_\mu^\top x \}.
\end{equation*}

In fact, one can take any $C_\mu$ such that $\ker C_\mu^\top = \pi (\mathcal{L})$. Conversely, any such equations for a symmetric map $Q: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{X}^*$ define a Lagrange structure.

\label{prop:monotoneaug}
Consider a maximally monotone structure $\mathcal{M} \subset \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X}^*$. Write $\pi^* \mathcal{M}= \ker C_\lambda^\top$ for some matrix $C_\lambda: \Lambda \to \mathcal{X}$ with full column rank and linear space $\Lambda$. Then there exists a monotone map $M: \mathcal{X}^* \to \mathcal{X}$ such that
\begin{equation*}
\label{eq:monrep}
\mathcal{M} = \left \{(f,e) \in \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X}^* \mid\ \exists\ e_\lambda \in \Lambda^* \mbox{ s.t. } \begin{bmatrix} f \\[2mm] 0 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -M & -C_\lambda \\[2mm] C_\lambda ^\top  & 0 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} e \\[2mm] e_\lambda \end{bmatrix} \right \}.
\end{equation*}
Conversely, for any monotone map $M$ this defines a maximally monotone structure.

\label{prop:nonldiracaug}
Consider any Dirac structure $\mathcal{D}$ on a manifold $\mathcal{X}$. Suppose that $\pi^*(\mathcal{D}(x)) \subset T_x\mathcal{X}^*$ is $(n-k)$-dimensional and set $\Lambda:= \mathbb{R}^k$. Then there exists a full-rank $n \times k$ matrix function $C_\lambda(x): \Lambda^* \to T_x\mathcal{X}$ and a skew-symmetric $n \times n$ matrix function $J(x)$ such that locally

\begin{equation}
\label{Diracconstraint2}
\begin{array} {l} \mathcal{D}(x) = \left \{(f,e) \! \in T_x\mathcal{X} \times T_x^*\mathcal{X} \mid \exists\, e_\lambda \in \Lambda^* \mbox{ s.t. }-f = J(x)e + C_\lambda(x)e_\lambda,\right .\\[2mm] \qquad \qquad\left . 0  =  C_\lambda^\top(x)e \right \}.
\end{array}
\end{equation}

Conversely, any such equations for a skew-symmetric map $J(x): T_x^*\mathcal{X} \to T_x\mathcal{X}$ define a Dirac structure.

\label{prop:nonllagrangeaug}
Consider a Lagrangian submanifold $\mathcal{L} \subset T^*\mathcal{X}$, a point $\ell \in \mathcal{L}$ and its projection $\pi(\ell) \in \mathcal{X}$. Then there exists a neighborhood $V$ of $\pi(\ell)$, a natural number $k$, a neighborhood $W$ of $0 \in \mathbb{R}^k$, together with a smooth function $F: V \times W \to \mathbb{R}$, such that the rank of $\frac{\partial F}{\partial \mu}$, with $\mu \in \mathbb{R}^k$, is equal to $k$ on $\left(\frac{\partial F}{\partial \mu}\right)^{-1}(0)$, and

\begin{equation}
\label{morse3}
\left \{ \left(x, \frac{\partial F}{\partial x}(x, \mu) \right) \mid \frac{\partial F}{\partial \mu}(x, \mu)=0 \right \}
\end{equation}

is a neighborhood of the point $\ell$ in $\mathcal{L}$. The function $F(x,\mu)$, seen as a function of $x$ and parametrized by $\mu \in \mathbb{R}^k$, is called a Morse family for the Lagrangian submanifold $\mathcal{L}$.
